interface.longDescription:
Preferly checks how AI assistants and AI search tools see a business website. Give it a website address and it can:

- ask 3 AI models typical customer questions about the business and show whether each one names it, where it ranks when it gives a list, and up to 2 places where an AI model contradicts the website;
- check which of 13 AI crawlers (search, assistant and training crawlers) the site's robots.txt allows or blocks, and the rule that decides each one, and whether the homepage loads for an automated visitor or shows a bot check from a service such as Cloudflare;
- check the site's llms.txt against the llmstxt.org format, line by line;
- read the business details in the homepage's structured data (schema.org JSON-LD): the business type, which fields are present, missing or look off, and where the structured data and the page disagree;
- check whether the business name, phone, email, address, hours and prices are readable as text, or only appear after scripts run;
- write robots.txt lines that let AI search and assistant crawlers in and keep AI training crawlers out, or any mix.

Each result starts with one plain sentence and lists what to look at next.

Who it's for: business owners, marketers and web developers who want to know whether AI tools can read a site.

How it works: the checks read public files only: robots.txt, the homepage, /llms.txt and up to 2 contact, booking or pricing pages that robots.txt allows. They don't log in, fill in forms or change anything on the site. Our crawler identifies itself as PreferlyBot. The AI model check is a quick preview: 3 AI models answer from what they already know, without web search, and a result for the same website may come from a check run in the last 24 hours.
